    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 0 & 0\\cdot 6 & 0\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot 0 & 4\\cdot 6 & 4\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & 0\\cdot 6 & 0\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & 0\\cdot 6 & 0\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 24 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
